  <div><p><strong>Practical discontinuation (hold) thresholds used in TB treatment (drug-susceptible TB disease):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>ALT ≥3× ULN WITH symptoms of hepatitis</strong> (e.g., anorexia, nausea/vomiting, RUQ pain/tenderness, fatigue) <strong>and/or jaundice</strong> → <strong>stop hepatotoxic drugs immediately</strong> (H, R, Z). <a href="https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciw566">5</a></li>
<li><strong>ALT ≥5× ULN WITHOUT symptoms</strong> → <strong>stop hepatotoxic drugs immediately</strong> (H, R, Z). <a href="https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciw566">5</a></li>
<li><strong>LTBI 3HP (rifapentine/INH):</strong> discontinue if <strong>AST ≥5× ULN without symptoms</strong> or <strong>≥3× ULN with symptoms</strong>. <a href="https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.mm6725a5">3</a></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>If you are using “clinical-trial style” DILI stopping rules (more conservative, FDA-type) sometimes applied to newer regimens:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Discontinue study drugs for any of: <strong>ALT/AST &gt;8× ULN; &gt;5× ULN for &gt;2 weeks; &gt;3× ULN with bilirubin &gt;2× ULN or INR &gt;1.5; or &gt;3× ULN with systemic symptoms/rash/eosinophilia</strong>. (Prospective phase 2b TB trial applying FDA guidance.) <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/s1473-3099(25)00213-0">1</a></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Important exception—tuberculous meningitis (TBM):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>In TBM, <strong>stopping anti‑TB drugs is an independent risk factor for death</strong>, so weigh interruption against DILI severity; an RCT in TBM found <strong>continuing all drugs unless ALT ≥10× ULN (or bilirubin ≥43 µmol/L or INR &gt;1.5 / worsening symptoms)</strong> reduced time off rifampicin/isoniazid without observed liver failure (nested RCT, <strong>67</strong> TBM patients with DILI). <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jinf.2026.106796">2</a></li>
<li>This supports considering <strong>higher stopping thresholds with very close monitoring</strong> in TBM specifically, but it is not yet the default standard for pulmonary TB. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jinf.2026.106796">2</a> <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/s1473-3099(25)00364-0">4</a></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>References:</strong></p><p>1. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciw566" style="text-decoration: underline; color: #1a0dab;">Executive Summary: Official American Thoracic Society/Centers for Disease Control and Prevention/Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guidelines: Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Tuberculosis</a> 2016. DOI: 10.1093/cid/ciw566</p><p>2. <a href="https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.mm6725a5" style="text-decoration: underline; color: #1a0dab;">Update of Recommendations for Use of Once-Weekly Isoniazid-Rifapentine Regimen to Treat LatentMycobacterium tuberculosisInfection</a> 2018. DOI: 10.15585/mmwr.mm6725a5</p><p>3. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/s1473-3099(25)00213-0" style="text-decoration: underline; color: #1a0dab;">Sutezolid in combination with bedaquiline, delamanid, and moxifloxacin for pulmonary tuberculosis (PanACEA-SUDOCU-01): a prospective, open-label, randomised, phase 2b dose-finding trial</a> 2025. DOI: 10.1016/s1473-3099(25)00213-0</p><p>4. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jinf.2026.106796" style="text-decoration: underline; color: #1a0dab;">A randomised comparison of management strategies for drug-induced liver injury associated with tuberculous meningitis treatment.</a> 2026. DOI: 10.1016/j.jinf.2026.106796</p><p>5. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/s1473-3099(25)00364-0" style="text-decoration: underline; color: #1a0dab;">A clinical practice guideline for tuberculous meningitis</a> 2025. DOI: 10.1016/s1473-3099(25)00364-0</p></div>
